    "create_time": 1763436772.87,
    "update_time": null,
    "content": {
     "content_type": "text",
     "parts": [
      "That's the classic boundary. An error on screen tells you a state started; it doesn't tell you how the student is responding. A rule that usually helps:\n\n**Decision rule for windows with an active error:**\n- **productive**: the student makes an edit that plausibly targets the error (touches the line in the traceback, or the function it names) within the window, or runs again after a targeted edit.\n- **stalled-after-error**: there's an error on screen and, within the window, *no edit related to it*: idle, scrolling unrelated code, re-running unchanged code, or tab-switching away.\n- If unsure → look at the next window. If the student fixes it there, the current window is productive.\n\nAdd 3–4 annotated example windows for each side to the guide, then have both relabel the 200 and recompute. A jump from 0.58 into the 0.7s after a guide revision is common."
     ]
    },
